{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794524381552,"sessionID":"ses_e2e0e4b1affe2HORa6vu9VcI7O","part":{"partID":"prt_1d1fcf34c002iD3oj22GHNuzO1","sessionID":"ses_e2e0e4b1affe2HORa6vu9VcI7O","messageID":"msg_1d1fcd74f001X0JcJz3NB6NS1Z","type":"tool","id":"call_3230bc03dc9d41b280ae0ec0","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -n \"hooks\\|features\\|trust\" .codex/config.toml | head -20; echo \"--- README approval\"; grep -n \"approve\\|hooks\" .codex/README.md | head -15","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research"},"output":"1:[features]\n3:hooks = true\n--- README approval\n3:description: \"How the Codex CLI uses this repository: the agents, prompts and hooks that generators write from .skilled/, how to approve the hooks and how to check that they are in sync.\"\n7:  - \"codex hooks approval\"\n12:> Codex reads this repository through agents, prompts and hooks that generators write from the shared source, so the same guards run in a Codex session.\n23:| **Start with** | Run `codex` in this checkout, then approve the project hooks once (section 3) |\n29:Codex cannot read `.skilled/` directly. It wants agents as TOML files, prompts as a flat folder and hooks in its own `hooks.json`. Generators translate the shared source into those formats, and this folder holds their output.\n31:Each generator deletes any output it no longer expects, so a removed command or agent leaves no stale copy behind. The `config.toml` file is the one file here that no generator writes. It turns hooks on and sets up the Code Mode server.\n43:| Hooks and guards | `hooks.json`, rendered from the shared hook registry. The hooks cover the advisor, the spec-folder gate, the dispatch and MCP route guards, the post-edit checks and the session context. Each command falls back to a message when its adapter is missing |\n53:1. Open the repository in Codex from its root. Codex reads `hooks.json` from this checkout once the checkout is trusted.\n54:2. Approve the hooks. Codex runs a project hook only after you approve that exact entry. Open `/hooks` in an interactive session and approve each entry it lists for review. The approval is stored as a `trusted_hash` in `~/.codex/config.toml`, so a changed entry needs approving again.\n55:3. Check the global file. `~/.codex/hooks.json` must hold no copy of these entries, or each hook runs twice. Run `install-codex-hooks.mjs --check` to confirm that it holds no stale copy.\n65:| `hooks.json` | Generated | Rendered from `hook-registry.json` by `sync-hook-registrations.cjs` |\n66:| `hooks/` | Links | A discovery mirror of the scripts that `hooks.json` runs |\n67:| `config.toml` | Authored | Turns hooks on and writes the Code Mode server inline. No generator writes it |\n71:**The one rule:** never edit a generated file here. Change the source in `.skilled/`, or change `hook-registry.json` for hooks, then rerun the matching generator. A hand edit to `hooks.json` is overwritten by the next sync.\n81:node .skilled/bin/install-codex-hooks.mjs --check\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1:[features]\n3:hooks = true\n--- README approval\n3:description: \"How the Codex CLI uses this repository: the agents, prompts and hooks that generators write from .skilled/, how to approve the hooks and how to check that they are in sync.\"\n7:  - \"codex hooks approval\"\n12:> Codex reads this repository through agents, prompts and hooks that generators write from the shared source, so the same guards run in a Codex session.\n23:| **Start with** | Run `codex` in this checkout, then approve the project hooks once (section 3) |\n29:Codex cannot read `.skilled/` directly. It wants agents as TOML files, prompts as a flat folder and hooks in its own `hooks.json`. Generators translate the shared source into those formats, and this folder holds their output.\n31:Each generator deletes any output it no longer expects, so a removed command or agent leaves no stale copy behind. The `config.toml` file is the one file here that no generator writes. It turns hooks on and sets up the Code Mode server.\n43:| Hooks and guards | `hooks.json`, rendered from the shared hook registry. The hooks cover the advisor, the spec-folder gate, the dispatch and MCP route guards, the post-edit checks and the session context. Each command falls back to a message when its adapter is missing |\n53:1. Open the repository in Codex from its root. Codex reads `hooks.json` from this checkout once the checkout is trusted.\n54:2. Approve the hooks. Codex runs a project hook only after you approve that exact entry. Open `/hooks` in an interactive session and approve each entry it lists for review. The approval is stored as a `trusted_hash` in `~/.codex/config.toml`, so a changed entry needs approving again.\n55:3. Check the global file. `~/.codex/hooks.json` must hold no copy of these entries, or each hook runs twice. Run `install-codex-hooks.mjs --check` to confirm that it holds no stale copy.\n65:| `hooks.json` | Generated | Rendered from `hook-registry.json` by `sync-hook-registrations.cjs` |\n66:| `hooks/` | Links | A discovery mirror of the scripts that `hooks.json` runs |\n67:| `config.toml` | Authored | Turns hooks on and writes the Code Mode server inline. No generator writes it |\n71:**The one rule:** never edit a generated file here. Change the source in `.skilled/`, or change `hook-registry.json` for hooks, then rerun the matching generator. A hand edit to `hooks.json` is overwritten by the next sync.\n81:node .skilled/bin/install-codex-hooks.mjs --check\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"grep -n \\\"hooks\\\\|features\\\\|trust\\\" .codex/config.toml | head -20; echo \\\"--- README approval\\\"; grep -n \\\"approve\\\\|hooks\\\" .codex/README.md | head -15\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research\"}"},"time":{"start":1794524377932,"end":1794524381552}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794524381627,"sessionID":"ses_e2e0e4b1affe2HORa6vu9VcI7O","part":{"partID":"prt_1d1fcf64e0012h80n63kDSnVoX","sessionID":"ses_e2e0e4b1affe2HORa6vu9VcI7O","messageID":"msg_1d1fcd74f001X0JcJz3NB6NS1Z","type":"tool","id":"call_d34f82211efa41788f00a733","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"A=/private/var/folders/3c/zfqcqsts0kn19cgblj82gqhm0000gn/T/skilled-behaviour-arenas/pre-codex; grep -n \"hook\\|trust\" \"$A/.codex/config.toml\" 2>/dev/null | head -10; echo \"--- codex home trust\"; grep -n \"trust\\|hooks\" ~/.codex/config.toml 2>/dev/null | head -10; echo \"--- session head keys\"; node -e '\nconst fs=require(\"fs\");const rows=fs.readFileSync(\"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/007-per-runtime-behaviour-proof/scratch/runs-pre.jsonl\",\"utf8\").trim().split(\"\\n\").map(JSON.parse).filter(r=>r.runtime===\"codex\"&&r.locator.transcriptPath);\nconst t=fs.readFileSync(rows[0].locator.transcriptPath,\"utf8\").split(\"\\n\").slice(0,6);\nfor(const l of t){try{const o=JSON.parse(l);console.log(o.type||Object.keys(o).slice(0,6).join(\",\"))}catch{console.log(\"nonjson\")}}\n'","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research"},"output":"3:hooks = true\n--- codex home trust\n12:trust_level = \"trusted\"\n15:trust_level = \"trusted\"\n18:trust_level = \"trusted\"\n21:trust_level = \"trusted\"\n24:trust_level = \"trusted\"\n27:trust_level = \"trusted\"\n30:trust_level = \"trusted\"\n33:trust_level = \"trusted\"\n36:trust_level = \"trusted\"\n39:trust_level = \"trusted\"\n--- session head keys\nsession_meta\nevent_msg\nresponse_item\nresponse_item\nresponse_item\nresponse_item\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3:hooks = true\n--- codex home trust\n12:trust_level = \"trusted\"\n15:trust_level = \"trusted\"\n18:trust_level = \"trusted\"\n21:trust_level =